Aim: To create, build and execute a Java application using Gradle in Ubuntu.
Steps to perform
1. Open Ubuntu terminal.
2. Install Gradle:
   sudo snap install gradle --classic
3. Create a directory:
   mkdir java_application
4. Enter the directory:
   cd java_application
5. Initialize a Gradle project:
   gradle init
6. Gradle will ask for configurations. Choose the options shown in your manual:
   
   | Configuration | Selection |
   |---|---|
   | Project type | Application |
   | Language | Java |
   | Java version | 17 |
   | Project name | java_application |
   | Structure | Single application |
   | DSL | Kotlin or Groovy |
   | Test framework | JUnit 4 |
7. Complete any remaining Gradle initialization prompts.
8. Install the tree command:
   sudo apt install tree -y
9. Run the generated application:
   ./gradlew run
10. Open the generated App.java file.
11. Replace the default Java code with the arithmetic program from your manual.
12. Check the package name in App.java.
13. Open build.gradle.kts or build.gradle, depending on the DSL you selected.
14. Set the application's mainClass to match the fully qualified name of your Java class.
15. Run the application again:
./gradlew run


16. View the project structure:
tree


17. Create a GitHub repository and push the Gradle project:
git init
git add .
git status
git commit -m "initial commit"
git branch -M main
git remote add origin YOUR_REPOSITORY_URL
git push -u origin main
